      "why": "iterative already owns 22/23 of the merged front and its best feasible area (6930 um2) is far below pipelined (31701) and pipelined_m (20020). Since area is the selection objective and the only hard constraints are throughput>=1 MSPS and max_abs_err<=2^-10, the whole front lives in the low-area iterative corner. I narrow to the region that actually produces feasible, low-area points: data_width 12-18 (12 is the smallest width that can reach ~10+ accuracy bits with enough n_iter; going below 12 risks missing the error bound, going above 18 only inflates area), n_iter 12-18 (enough micro-rotations to hit 2^-10 with modest width; beyond ~18 area grows with little accuracy gain), angle_guard -2..2 (negative guard shrinks the atan-LUT/angle path and area; -2 is the registry floor), frac_guard 0..2 (0 is cheapest; small extra guard only trades area for a tiny accuracy bump), rounding both (round can shave the error term, letting a slightly smaller n_iter/width stay feasible). This focuses the remaining budget on densely mapping the iterative trade-off curve rather than re-exploring wide ranges."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "unrolled_k is the only other family I may use, and it is the natural area/throughput trade-off partner to iterative: chaining k micro-rotations per cycle raises throughput (helping the >=1 MSPS constraint at lower Fmax) but adds combinational depth and area. I keep the same accuracy-relevant ranges as iterative (data_width 12-18, n_iter 12-18, angle_guard -2..2, frac_guard 0..2, both roundings) so the accuracy axis is comparable, and restrict k to 2..4: k=2 is the cheapest unrolled option and most likely to stay near iterative's area while still clearing 1 MSPS; k>4 tends to add area and timing pressure with diminishing throughput benefit for this low-throughput spec. This lets the search confirm whether any unrolled_k point beats or complements the iterative front, and if not, the merged front stays dominated by iterative."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The fleet facts show the merged front is essentially the iterative family (22/23 points, best area 6930 um2), while pipelined (31701) and pipelined_m (20020) are far larger in area and contribute almost nothing. Since the spec's selection rule is min area and the only hard constraints are throughput>=1 MSPS and max_abs_err<=2^-10, the value of the remaining budget is to densely map the low-area iterative trade-off curve (accuracy_bits vs area) and to check whether unrolled_k offers any area/throughput point that iterative misses. I therefore concentrate on iterative with tight, feasibility-focused ranges (small data_width 12-18, n_iter 12-18, negative angle_guard down to -2, minimal frac_guard, both roundings) to push area down while keeping the error bound, and include unrolled_k with the same accuracy ranges plus k=2..4 to test the throughput/area trade-off. I avoid wide ranges that would waste evaluations on infeasible or area-dominated corners, and I do not touch pipelined/pipelined_m since they are outside my assigned families and already dominated."
}

